# Code owners for couchsophia1963-hub/belmont.
#
# BEL-223. This file exists for one reason: to make `main` unmergeable by the
# author of a pull request.
#
# The measured problem. As of 2026-10-05T17:52Z this repository has merged 28
# pull requests. 27 of them were authored by EasySchedule and merged by
# EasySchedule. The 28th, PR #1, was the only one merged by a second identity.
# Across all 28 there are zero approving reviews. The three reviews that exist
# on the open queue are all `COMMENTED`, and all three are from EasySchedule on
# pull requests EasySchedule authored. So the history is not a near-miss; the
# approval gate has never once been satisfied.
#
# This count is not stable, which is the point. The first measurement of this
# comment, taken seven minutes earlier at 17:45Z, was 27 merged and 26
# self-merged. PR #39 was then merged by EasySchedule on a branch EasySchedule
# authored, with zero reviews, at 17:47:57Z -- while this pull request was being
# written. The gap does not close on its own.
#
# Why a count of 1 is enough, and why this file is still required. GitHub
# refuses to let an author approve their own pull request at all -- not "the
# approval does not count", but a hard refusal:
#
#   POST /repos/couchsophia1963-hub/belmont/pulls/42/reviews  (event=APPROVE)
#   -> 422 Unprocessable Entity
#      "Review Can not approve your own pull request"
#
# reproduced on 2026-10-05 against PRs #42 and #40, and matched by GraphQL on
# #40. So `required_approving_review_count: 1` already forces a second GitHub
# identity. Two reviewers are not required and are not wanted: there are only
# two identities with write on this repository, so requiring 2 approvals would
# deadlock the repo permanently rather than merely slow it.
#
# What this file adds on top of the count. The count says "somebody who is not
# the author". It does not say *which* somebody, and with two identities that
# is not a meaningful ambiguity -- but it would become one the moment a third
# login is added. Naming the owner here means the approving identity is written
# down in the repository instead of being implied by whoever happens to hold
# write. It also makes `require_code_owner_review: true` in the ruleset
# satisfiable; without this file that setting has nothing to match and would
# block every pull request.
#
# THE SINGLE POINT OF FAILURE, stated plainly: every merge to `main` now
# depends on one human login. `dustinwloring1988` is the only collaborator here
# who is not EasySchedule, and the only person who can satisfy this. That person
# being unavailable means `main` is blocked. That is the intended trade -- a
# blocked `main` is the correct outcome when no second pair of eyes exists --
# but it is a real cost and it is the thing to revisit if this becomes a
# bottleneck. The fix for it is more identities, not a weaker rule.
#
# `*` covers every path. There is no intent here to exempt `docs/**` or
# `supabase/**`: the evidence that motivated BEL-223 includes self-merges of
# database migrations, where an unreviewed change is least recoverable.

* @dustinwloring1988